---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { APP_STORE_URL, UPDATED } from "../config";
import { path } from "../i18n/ui";

const description =
  "Open and close your garage door from iPhone, Apple Watch, widgets, Siri or the Action Button, using a UniFi Protect relay and door sensor. Verified by the sensor, no hub, no subscription.";

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "SoftwareApplication",
  name: "Garage Opener",
  applicationCategory: "LifestyleApplication",
  operatingSystem: "iOS, watchOS",
  description,
  url: "https://garageopener.app",
  inLanguage: ["en-US", "fr-FR"],
  offers: { "@type": "Offer", price: "0", priceCurrency: "USD" },
};
---

<Base locale="en" route="home" title="A real smart garage door for UniFi Protect" description={description} jsonLd={jsonLd} updated={UPDATED}>
  <section class="hero">
    <span class="badge">Works with UniFi Protect</span>
    <h1>Your garage door, finally on your phone.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      You already have a UniFi Protect console, a relay wired to the opener and a sensor on the door.
      Garage Opener turns those into a proper smart garage door — on your iPhone, your Apple Watch, your Lock
      Screen and in Siri. No hub. No subscription. No Home Assistant.
    </p>

    <div class="actions">
      {APP_STORE_URL
        ? <a class="btn" href={APP_STORE_URL}>Download on the App Store</a>
        : <span class="btn ghost" aria-disabled="true">Coming to the App Store</span>}
      <a class="btn ghost" href={path("en", "faq")}>How it works</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>The sensor decides, not the app</h2>
    <p>
      Most garage integrations fire the opener and then assume. This one doesn't. Every command is verified
      against the door sensor before the app tells you it worked, so <em>open</em> means the door is open —
      not that a relay clicked. When the door doesn't do what it was asked, you get told, clearly.
    </p>

    <h2>Three ways to run it</h2>
    <p>Pick one during setup. You can change your mind later.</p>

    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Connect to Protect directly</h3>
        <p>
          The app talks to your console on your home network. Nothing else to install, nothing else to keep
          running. The simplest setup, and the right one for most people.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Run the bridge</h3>
        <p>
          A small service on a NAS, a Pi or any box with Docker. Adds always-on alert rules, an activity log
          you can export, family invites and Shortcuts that work when your phone is asleep.
          <a href={path("en", "selfHosting")}>Self-hosting guide →</a>
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Cloud alerts</h3>
        <p>
          No bridge, but you still want to be told when the door is left open. Your console posts door events
          to a small service we run; it pushes a notification back. It never holds your Protect key.
          <a href={path("en", "privacy")}>What it stores →</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>What you actually get</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Everywhere you'd want it</strong> — iPhone, Apple Watch, widgets, Control Center, the Action Button, Siri and Shortcuts.</li>
      <li><strong>A Live Activity while the door is open</strong>, with a Close button on the Lock Screen.</li>
      <li><strong>Told when it matters</strong> — open too long, still open at night, or a car in the garage with the door up. Hold it open when you mean to.</li>
      <li><strong>Arriving home</strong> gets you a notification with an Open button. Nothing ever opens by itself.</li>
      <li><strong>Stop part-way</strong>, and the next press goes the way you expect.</li>
      <li><strong>Family without secrets</strong> — other phones join with a one-time invite, and you can see and revoke them.</li>
      <li><strong>English and French</strong>, throughout.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Try it before you wire anything</h2>
    <p>
      Demo mode runs the whole app against a simulated door — travel time, sensor, alerts, the lot. No
      console, no credentials, no hardware. It's the same mode App Review uses.
    </p>

    <h2>What you need</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>A UniFi Protect console</td><td>Tested on Protect 7.2.x. A UDM, Cloud Key or Dream Machine — anything that runs Protect and offers an integration API key.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>A relay with an output set to <strong>Pulse</strong></td><td>Wired across your opener's push-button terminals, exactly like the wall button.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>An all-in-one sensor, mounted as <strong>Garage</strong></td><td>This is the part that makes it honest — it's what reports whether the door is really open.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>An iPhone on a recent iOS</td><td>Apple Watch optional.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Setting the hardware up is covered step by step in the
      <a href={path("en", "selfHosting")}>guide</a>, and the <a href={path("en", "faq")}>FAQ</a> answers the
      questions people actually hit — starting with why the relay seems to "toggle".
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
